WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.169
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy-winning writer as well as

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.104
an Emmy- and
Golden Globe-nominated actor

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:08.441
you know from shows like
"Mr. Show" and "Breaking Bad,"

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:10.143
as well as the film "Nobody."

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:13.746
His memoir, "Comedy Comedy
Comedy Drama" is out now,

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:15.915
and he stars in the sixth
and final season of

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:19.018
"Better Call Saul," which airs
Mondays on AMC and AMC+.

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:20.253
Let's take a look.

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:22.054
-Here at Saul Goodman
and Associates,

00:00:22.054 --> 00:00:26.125
we welcome all ages, all creeds,
all stripes.

00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:29.462
-And who is this Saul Goodman?

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:31.864
You?
-You got it.

00:00:31.864 --> 00:00:33.833
What Colonel Sanders
is to chicken,

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.935
Saul Goodman is to the law.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.972
-Please welcome back to the show
Bob Odenkirk, everybody!

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:41.207
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:47.713 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:54.220 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.122
Welcome back, Bob!

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:59.626
We've got a lot to talk about.

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:00.827
[ Cheers and applause continue ]
-Are you kidding me?

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:04.264
What's going on?
What is going on?

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:08.067
Alright.
-This is --

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.969
-But wait, wait.
If you're watching this show,

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:12.372
are you watching
"Better Call Saul," too?

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:14.807
-Oh, 'cause --
Well, no, this is on after.

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:16.142
-Oh, okay.
-Yeah.

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:17.977
So you're safe. Don't worry.
-We're all safe.

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:22.248
-There's no -- There's no show
of "Saul's" quality

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:23.949
that would ever air
this late at night.

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:26.485
[ Laughter ]
Congratulations, by the way.

00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:28.154
-Thank you.
-You got a star.

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:30.823
-I got a star on the
Hollywood Walk of Fame.

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:33.225
[ Cheers and applause ]
Look at this.

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:37.296
All these -- All these people
have killed each other

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:40.066
or might kill each other
on television.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.235
-It's a real reminder
that TV is not real.

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:44.537
-It was so much fun,
and we're such good friends.

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:45.638
-There's your beautiful wife.

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.273
And I'm assuming that's
your dog as well.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.674
-My wife, Naomi.

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:52.678
And that's Winnie, our dog,
who we adopted during COVID.

00:01:52.678 --> 00:01:55.414
-Oh, wow.
-And now lives next door,

00:01:55.414 --> 00:01:57.616
so she comes over all the time.
-That's great.

00:01:57.616 --> 00:01:59.785
-We're her grandparents.
-Was this the first time

00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:02.255
this adopted dog realized
what a star you were?

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:03.856
Is that why you brought it?

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:07.293
-No, I lectured her every day
from the time we got her.

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:09.595
-Yeah.
-That was really fun,

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:13.866
and Hollywood never feels like
a small town so much as those

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:16.169
Hollywood Walk of Fame
ceremonies.

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:18.337
It is darling.
-It's true.

00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:20.173
I don't think people quite --

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:21.674
I mean, it's not like
some massive si--

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.611
-Well, you have people from
the Chamber of Commerce,

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:26.578
and they're really proud,
and there's a feeling of

00:02:26.578 --> 00:02:30.350
like, "And we have someone here
who made -- He's in TV."

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.117
[ Laughter ]
"Yeah, I know!

00:02:32.117 --> 00:02:34.553
We're in Hollywood." What?

00:02:34.553 --> 00:02:38.457
They're so happy and proud
for you, and it's amazing.

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:40.260
-That's really great.
-You'd be very happy.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.094
-I obviously,
like a lot of people,

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:43.996
knew you first as a comedian.

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:45.798
Now, it should be noted,
I also knew you as somebody

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:48.767
who approached comedy with
the same rigor and took it

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:50.803
as seriously as anybody
who does dramatic acting.

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:53.139
-Yes.
-But drama did occur to you

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:55.307
in your early days of comedy
that you might do it.

00:02:55.307 --> 00:02:58.511
-Yeah. I mean, it was kind of
a brain-fart moment.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:02.515
I was on stage with Chris Farley
and Jill Talley at Second City,

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:07.486
and we were improvising a scene,
and Chris was always so funny.

00:03:07.486 --> 00:03:11.256
And I'm in the middle of
this scene, but you know

00:03:11.256 --> 00:03:13.159
what it's like when you do
the Second City show

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:15.027
and you do seven shows a week

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:17.530
and you can think about,
like, groceries.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.865
[ Sighs ]
"I got to get eggs later,"

00:03:19.865 --> 00:03:22.134
and then you're your character
and then you're like,

00:03:22.134 --> 00:03:26.405
"Ah, I've also got to get, you
know, some new socks tomorrow."

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:30.809
Anyway, Chris -- You can think
about a million things,

00:03:30.809 --> 00:03:36.082
and Chris and I are acting and I
think, "I should be in a drama."

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:37.783
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:39.418
"That's what I should do."

00:03:39.418 --> 00:03:41.787
And I really had
that crazy thought.

00:03:41.787 --> 00:03:45.891
I was just -- I think I was just
loving his presence so much

00:03:45.891 --> 00:03:48.527
and he was so wonderful,
and I thought,

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:50.095
"I can't compete with that."
-Yes.

00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:53.132
-But put me in a drama,
and I'll be so funny.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:58.271
-I worked with a lot of people
where I thought that as well.

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:01.807
-Well, there's a lot of people
who are so undeniable, you know,

00:04:01.807 --> 00:04:04.944
that we all know, you know --
Jack Black, Chris Farley,

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:06.279
people I've worked with.

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:09.081
I think David Cross
is always funny.

00:04:09.081 --> 00:04:12.751
And so, you know, I had that
thought, but I never pursued it

00:04:12.751 --> 00:04:15.988
because I already started my
life as a comedy writer

00:04:15.988 --> 00:04:19.091
and I wasn't going to go back in
time and take some,

00:04:19.091 --> 00:04:21.627
what, acting classes.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:23.796
Who needs that?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:26.332
-But this was the situation with
"Saul."

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.867
You know, they called up, right?

00:04:28.867 --> 00:04:31.371
Vince knew your work
as a comedian.

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:33.472
-Yeah, it's so weird
because I was writing

00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:35.440
and I got a phone call
during "Breaking Bad."

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.177
They asked me, "If you want to
play this sleazy lawyer,"

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:40.346
and I said,
"Well, I can do that."

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:42.381
But I'd never seen
"Breaking Bad"

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:44.517
and I didn't watch
"Breaking Bad."

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:47.453
I went and flew to Albuquerque
to be on it.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:49.388
I watched like 10 minutes of it.

00:04:49.388 --> 00:04:51.390
"It's a drama. Okay, cool."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.593
And all I'd seen
are the billboards.

00:04:53.593 --> 00:04:56.496
All I knew was guy in the desert
in underwear.

00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:58.464
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:03.803
So I get there and -- And I have
to dial it in like that.

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:05.571
But I have me and Cranston.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:10.075
I'm working with Cranston, and
so I immediately just honed in

00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:12.544
on his --
He was gravity, you know?

00:05:12.544 --> 00:05:16.949
He was powerful in that role
and every role he does,

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:20.353
and he just brought me into
the orbit of that show.

00:05:20.353 --> 00:05:23.656
And from that point on, I --
it was great fun.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.958
And then "Saul" was
a whole nother level.

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:28.060
-Did you pretend that
you had seen the show?

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.630
-I pretended that I was talented
and I pretended that

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:35.935
I belonged there and I am
continuing to pretend that.

00:05:35.935 --> 00:05:37.836
And they gave me a star
because of it.

00:05:37.836 --> 00:05:40.973
-Yeah.
I mean, it must have been --

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:43.008
I mean, we talked about it
because, you know,

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:45.277
I had reached out to you during
"Better Call Saul" -- I'm sorry,

00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:47.113
excuse me -- during "Breaking
Bad" about how great you were.

00:05:47.113 --> 00:05:48.881
-You have been so kind.

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:50.616
Let's hear it for this guy.

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:52.651
-Oh, no. He's the best.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:52.651 --> 00:05:55.621
-He -- Listen.

00:05:55.621 --> 00:06:00.526
He has been so nice to me and
so appreciative of our show.

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:05.164
And you, of course, you know,
350 people make that show.

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:07.166
And they devote themselves
to it.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:09.368
And so thank you
on behalf of all of us.

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:12.238
-Well, let me just say, like,
there's never -- there are very

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:15.341
few shows where I feel like you
see the work of 350 people.

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:17.477
And this is a perfectly
constructed show.

00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:19.345
It is so well-written,
it is so well-acted,

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:20.813
and it looks really beautiful.

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:24.884
Yet with that said, you must
have had hesitation or thought,

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:27.119
"Is this --?" Because "Breaking
Bad" had this wonderful ending.

00:06:27.119 --> 00:06:29.621
It seemed like it was
completely, the loop was closed.

00:06:29.621 --> 00:06:30.690
And they were like,
"We're going to spin off."

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.325
-I should have had hesitation.
-Yeah.

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:33.992
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.992 --> 00:06:35.962
-But I got something wrong
with me.

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:37.229
-Yeah.

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:40.966
-I'm missing a part of my brain,
the part that says,

00:06:40.966 --> 00:06:42.301
"Hold up, slow down."

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:43.769
[ Laughter ]
"Wait a second.

00:06:43.769 --> 00:06:45.671
What are we going to do?"
-Well, we're all the better

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:47.072
that you lack
that part of your brain.

00:06:47.072 --> 00:06:50.075
I have a few more questions
about where that lack of --

00:06:50.075 --> 00:06:52.511
that part of your brain lacking
has come in handy.

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:54.580
We'll be right back with more
from Bob Odenkirk, everybody.

